## Sample employee gross-to-net
Jan Peeters, white-collar, PC 200, single/no dependants, full-time 38h, Berchem (Antwerpen) — profile chosen to mirror **CCG Module 12.1 Example 1** so the payslip reconciles line-for-line with the CCG's own worked example. July 2026: 22 working days, 21 worked + 1 public holiday (21 July, a Tuesday).

| Step | Calculation | EUR |
|---|---|---|
| 62100 Basic salary | 3,500.00 / 22 × 21 | 3,340.91 |
| 62964 Public holiday pay | 3,500.00 / 22 × 1 | 159.09 |
| **Gross (NSSO + taxable base)** | | **3,500.00** |
| 21900 Employee NSSO | 3,500.00 × 13.07% | −457.45 |
| BE-002 Social work bonus | ref. wage 3,500.00 > limit 3,403.62 | 0.00 |
| Taxable base | | 3,042.55 |
| Annualised ×12 | | 36,510.60 |
| Lump-sum prof. expenses | 30% = 10,953.18, capped | −6,070.00 |
| Annual net taxable | | 30,440.60 |
| Slice 1 | 16,710.00 × 26.75% | 4,469.93 |
| Slice 2 | 12,790.00 × 42.80% | 5,474.12 |
| Slice 3 | 940.60 × 48.15% | 452.90 |
| Annual tax before reductions | | 10,396.95 |
| Tax-free allowance reduction | 11,170.00 × 26.75% | −2,987.98 |
| Annual withholding | | 7,408.97 |
| BE-004 Monthly withholding | / 12 | −617.41 |
| BE-001 Special SS contribution | Q wage 10,500.00 → 30.99 + 1.10% × 3,929.46 = 74.21/qtr | −24.74 |
| BE-011 Meal voucher employee part | 21 × 1.09 | −22.89 |
| **Net paid** | | **2,377.51** |

Employer cost 5,195.03 (875.00 NSSO + 187.11 voucher employer part + 268.33 double-holiday provision + 291.67 end-of-year provision + 72.92 contributions on that provision).

## Independent recomputation
Two checks, neither reading the build script: a minimal formula evaluator walks the workbook's own `ROUND/MIN/MAX/SUM/IF` graph, and a separate routine recomputes the Belgian 2026 gross-to-net from the CCG parameters in plain Python. Both were compared against the CCG's published Example 1 figures. **43 comparisons per sheet, both sheets, 0 failures** (`_verify_payslip.py`). The evaluator also caught two real bugs before delivery: an off-by-one in the mid-block tax references and a malformed YTD reference.

Note: no Excel or LibreOffice is installed here, so openpyxl wrote formulas without cached results. I set `fullCalcOnLoad`, so Excel/LibreOffice recalculates the whole workbook on first open.

